# TicketDrop architectural boundaries

Read `ARCHITECTURE.md` before proposing cross-service changes.

## Product boundary

TicketDrop is an RA-only, alert-only onsale-signal product. A user selects one
configured RA metro and saves direct artist or venue labels. The system may
enumerate and poll RA, classify observable sale-state changes, and send links.
It must not fetch seller pages, enter queues, solve CAPTCHAs, submit payments,
or evade account or purchase limits.

## Service ownership

- `ingest-service/` — Go 1.25 and `net/http`. The only caller of RA and the only
  writer to Redis Stream `ticket:drops`. Owns catalogue enumeration,
  availability polling, fingerprints, classification, catalogue tables, and
  `event_snapshots`.
- `agent-harness/` — Python 3.12+ async service. The only consumer-group worker
  for `ticket:drops`. Owns scout matching, subscription reconciliation,
  `watchlist`, `drops`, `drop_transitions`, and notification delivery. It never
  polls RA.
- `admin-panel/` — Next.js App Router and Tailwind. Owns authenticated settings,
  direct `user_preferences` writes, explicit Watch/Unwatch, personal read
  models, and operator controls. Recent alerts and drops come from Postgres;
  Redis is read only for stream health.
- `db/` — the schema is the contract between services. Document cross-service
  ownership changes in `ARCHITECTURE.md`.

Go normally owns `venues`, `artists`, `events`, `event_artists`,
`event_snapshots`, and `ingest_watermarks`. Python owns `watchlist`,
`event_subscriptions`, `drops`, `drop_transitions`, and
`notification_deliveries`. Next.js owns user settings and preferences and may
write explicit subscriptions.

Python may upsert a stub `events` row when a valid stream message names a
missing event. The upsert must be conflict-safe, log a warning, and mark
`stubbed_event` in transition detail so Go can correct it on its next catalogue
upsert.

## Hard invariants

- Redis Streams is the only ingest-to-worker channel. No direct service call or
  shared in-process state.
- Postgres is durable. Redis contains the event log, poll snapshot cache, and
  short-lived claim keys. Redis loss may repeat work but must not create a
  second drop row or intentional second email.
- `drops` stays unique on `(event_id, fingerprint)`.
- Deliveries stay unique on `(drop_id, user_id, channel)`, and retries reuse
  `drop-<drop_id>-user-<user_id>-email`.
- Reconciliation creates one shared watch row per RA event regardless of
  subscriber count.
- Unwatch sets `active = false` and `user_disabled = true`; the scout must not
  reactivate that row.
- Artist and venue matching uses normalized full names, not fuzzy matching.
- Secrets come from the environment and blocking calls have explicit timeouts.

## Detection semantics

The first RA observation is a silent baseline. Publish only a classified later
change: presale activation, ticketed false-to-true, resale false-to-true, or
normalized cost change while ticketed. `dateUpdated` participates in the
fingerprint but never classifies a change alone. Exclude `attending` and
`interestedCount` from the fingerprint.

## Latency and testing

- Keep the RA poll interval configurable with a 500 ms floor. Production starts
  at 5 seconds, six workers, and one metro.
- Treat poll delay and stream-to-email latency as separate measurements.
- Every terminal worker outcome appends `drop_transitions` with measured queue
  lag and classification detail.
- Go tests are table-driven and use local doubles for network and stream work.
- Python coroutine tests use `pytest-asyncio`; cover claim, reclaim,
  dead-letter, duplicate fingerprint, disabled subscription, and email
  idempotency behavior.
- Network access in tests must be faked or explicitly isolated as an integration
  check.
